/* =====================================================================
   ANCIENT BRITAIN — c. 4000 BC to AD 410

   The source problem here is the sharpest in the app. Britain produced
   no history of itself before the Roman conquest and very little after
   it. Everything written about the island for four thousand years was
   written by outsiders — a Greek explorer his own countrymen called a
   liar, a Roman general with an election to win, a governor's
   son-in-law, and finally a British monk raging about a collapse he
   had not witnessed.

   So this Set leans harder than the others on things that are not
   texts: pollen cores, tree rings, isotopes in tooth enamel, ancient
   DNA, and the rubbish people left in ditches. Where the archaeology
   and the Roman account disagree, the chapters say so.

   Battles are briefed and handed off. The Roman conquest belongs in a
   war entry once the Roman Empire Set exists; these chapters stay on
   the British side of it.
   ===================================================================== */

CHAPTERS.push(
{
  id: "brit-stones", set: "ancient-britain", act: "Before Writing", title: "The Stones and the People Who Vanished", era: "c. 4000 – 1500 BC", minutes: 9,
  intro: "Britain's most famous monument was built by a population that was then almost entirely replaced by another — and the newcomers went on using it.",
  beats: [
    { title: "An island, and then farmers",
      text: ["Britain has not always been an island. Until about 6200 BC it was the north-west corner of a continental plain, and the land now under the southern North Sea — **Doggerland** — was river valleys, marsh and hunting ground. Trawlers still bring up its bones and flint tools. It drowned by degrees as the ice sheets melted, and the last of it seems to have gone under a tsunami thrown up by a submarine landslide off Norway, the **Storegga Slide**, which put a wave into eastern Britain that left a sand layer geologists can still point at.",
        "The people living here at that point were hunters and gatherers who had been in north-west Europe for thousands of years. The best-known of them is **Cheddar Man**, who died in a Somerset gorge around 7100 BC, and whose genome — published in 2018 — indicates dark skin with pale eyes, a combination that surprised people who had assumed the two travelled together.",
        "Around 4000 BC farming arrives: wheat and barley, cattle and sheep, pottery, and a completely different way of living on the land. For most of the twentieth century the argument was whether this was migration or fashion — whether new people came, or existing people adopted new ideas from across the Channel.",
        "Ancient DNA settled it, and not gently. The Neolithic population of Britain descends overwhelmingly from farmers whose ancestry traces back through Iberia to the Aegean. The hunter-gatherer contribution to the gene pool afterwards is very small. This is **Established** as a genetic result; what it meant on the ground — whether the earlier population was displaced, absorbed, or simply outnumbered over centuries — is not, and the honest answer is **Unknown**."],
      key: "Britain became an island around 6200 BC; farming arrived c. 4000 BC with a population largely descended from Aegean farmers." },
    { title: "What they built before Stonehenge",
      text: ["The first monuments are not stone circles. They are **long barrows** — communal tombs where bones were sorted, stacked and rearranged, sometimes for generations, so that the dead ended up filed by body part rather than by person. At **West Kennet** the chambers stayed in use for a thousand years before being deliberately blocked. Alongside them come **causewayed enclosures**: rings of ditch dug in segments, with gaps left between them, filled with feasting debris, broken pots and occasionally human remains.",
        "The single most precisely dated structure of British prehistory is not a monument at all. The **Sweet Track** is a raised wooden walkway across the Somerset marshes, and because its timbers survived waterlogged, tree-ring dating puts its construction in the winter of **3807 or 3806 BC** — a single season, nearly six thousand years ago. Beside it someone dropped a jadeite axe from the Italian Alps, unused, too fine to be a tool.",
        "The most surprising place to look for the origins of any of this is the far north. On **Orkney**, at the **Ness of Brodgar**, excavation has uncovered a walled complex of monumental buildings in use for a thousand years, with painted stonework and a roof of stone slabs, standing between two great circles. The village at **Skara Brae** preserves stone dressers, beds and hearths because there was no wood to build with. And the pottery style called **Grooved Ware**, which turns up later across Britain including at Stonehenge, appears to have originated in Orkney and spread south.",
        "That is worth pausing on. The conventional picture puts innovation in the fertile south and treats the far north as a periphery receiving it. For several centuries in the third millennium BC the traffic seems to have run the other way."],
      key: "Long barrows, causewayed enclosures and the Sweet Track precede Stonehenge; Grooved Ware pottery spread south from Orkney." },
    { title: "Stonehenge",
      text: ["Stonehenge was a cemetery before it was a stone circle. The earliest phase, around **3000 BC**, is a circular bank and ditch dug with antler picks, with a ring of pits — the **Aubrey Holes** — inside it. Those pits held cremated human bone, and the site as a whole has produced the largest known Neolithic cremation cemetery in Britain: the remains of perhaps a hundred and fifty people, buried over five centuries.",
        "The stones come later, around **2500 BC**, and they come from two very different places. The enormous sarsens were long assumed to be local; geochemical fingerprinting published in 2020 traced all but two of them to **West Woods**, about fifteen miles north near Marlborough. The smaller **bluestones** come from the **Preseli** hills in south-west Wales, roughly a hundred and forty miles away — and excavation at the outcrops of Craig Rhos-y-felin and Carn Goedog has found the quarry faces, with the stone extracted centuries before it was erected in Wiltshire. At **Waun Mawn** in Preseli there is a dismantled circle of the right diameter with an empty socket that fits one of the Stonehenge bluestones. Whether a Welsh monument was taken down and carried east is **Probable** rather than certain, and it is being argued about now.",
        "A mile and a half away at **Durrington Walls** lived the people who were doing the building, and their rubbish is unusually informative. Isotope analysis of the pig and cattle bones from their feasts shows animals raised in Scotland, Wales and the west of England and brought to Salisbury Plain to be eaten. People were not merely visiting from across Britain; they were bringing their livestock hundreds of miles to slaughter it here.",
        "The alignment is real and it is double. Stand in the centre at midsummer and the sun rises along the avenue; stand at the other end of the same axis at midwinter and it sets between the tallest stones. Which of the two mattered more to the builders is **Contested**, though the Durrington feasting debris points to midwinter gatherings."],
      key: "Stonehenge began c. 3000 BC as a cremation cemetery; the sarsens came from West Woods and the bluestones from Preseli in Wales." },
    { title: "The people who replaced them",
      text: ["Around **2450 BC**, within a century or two of the great stones going up, a new burial style appears: single graves, crouched bodies, distinctive decorated pots archaeologists call **Beakers**, and with them copper, gold and archery equipment.",
        "One of these graves was found in 2002 at Amesbury, three miles from Stonehenge. The man in it was buried with five Beaker pots, sixteen flint arrowheads, copper knives, a cushion stone for metalworking and two gold hair ornaments — the earliest gold yet found in Britain. The oxygen isotopes in his tooth enamel say he grew up somewhere with colder winters than Britain: the Alps, most likely. He had a badly injured knee and had walked or been carried a very long way. He is known as the **Amesbury Archer**.",
        "The genetic evidence around this period is startling and it is why he matters. Ancient DNA indicates that within a few centuries of the Beaker arrival, something like ninety per cent of the British gene pool had turned over, replaced by ancestry ultimately deriving from the steppe by way of central Europe. This is **Established** as a pattern in the data. *Why* is not: migration on a large scale, disease, violence, a collapse in the existing population's numbers, or some combination — the evidence cannot currently choose between them, and anyone who tells you confidently which it was has gone beyond it.",
        "The newcomers did not knock Stonehenge down. They rearranged it, added to it, and were buried around it for another five hundred years, including the man in the **Bush Barrow** grave with his gold lozenge and his mace. A monument outlived the entire population that conceived it and went on meaning something to people who had no ancestral claim to it whatsoever — which should be held in mind every time someone explains what Stonehenge was *for*.",
        "One thing it was not for: druids. No ancient source connects them to it. The idea belongs to the antiquarian **John Aubrey** in the seventeenth century and was popularised by **William Stukeley** in the eighteenth, and the gap between the last stone raised and the first druid any Greek or Roman mentions is around two thousand years — longer than the gap between us and the Roman conquest. It is classified here as **Traditional / Legendary**, and the tradition is Georgian."],
      key: "Beaker people arrived c. 2450 BC and the gene pool turned over almost entirely; they reused Stonehenge rather than abandoning it." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "What does ancient DNA show about Britain after about 2450 BC?", options: ["The population was unchanged", "Roughly 90% of the gene pool was replaced within a few centuries", "Farming was abandoned", "Stonehenge was destroyed"], correct: 1,
      explain: "The genetic turnover is established; the cause — migration, disease, violence or collapse — is not." },
    { q: "Where did Stonehenge's bluestones come from?", options: ["Salisbury Plain", "The Preseli hills in Wales", "Orkney", "Brittany"], correct: 1,
      explain: "The quarry faces have been excavated, and the stone was extracted centuries before it was raised in Wiltshire." },
    { q: "How should the link between druids and Stonehenge be classified?", options: ["Established", "Probable", "A Georgian antiquarian idea, roughly two thousand years too late", "Contested"], correct: 2,
      explain: "No ancient source makes the connection; Aubrey proposed it and Stukeley popularised it." },
  ],
},

{
  id: "brit-metal", set: "ancient-britain", act: "Before Writing", title: "Tin, Bronze and a System That Failed", era: "c. 2200 – 700 BC", minutes: 7,
  intro: "Britain had both metals bronze needs, which made a wet island at the edge of the world worth sailing to.",
  beats: [
    { title: "The reason anyone came",
      text: ["Bronze is copper with about a tenth part tin, and the difficulty is that the two almost never occur together. Most of Bronze Age Europe had to import one or the other across long distances. Britain had both — copper in Wales, Ireland and the south-west, and in **Cornwall** and Devon some of the most accessible tin deposits in the ancient world, lying in stream gravels where it could be washed out without mining at all.",
        "The scale of the copper working is easy to underestimate. At the **Great Orme** in north Wales, Bronze Age miners followed the ore into the limestone along more than five miles of passages, some of them narrow enough that the people who cut them can only have been children. The tools were stone hammers and bone scrapers, and the mine was worked for something like a thousand years.",
        "So Britain enters the European economy not as a curiosity at the edge of the map but as a supplier of something everybody needed. The boats that carried it are known too: the **Dover Boat**, a sewn-plank vessel of about 1550 BC, big enough to cross the Channel with cargo, and the wreck sites off **Salcombe** and **Langdon Bay** where cargoes of continental bronze went down within sight of the shore."],
      key: "Britain held both copper and tin, making it a source for a metal the whole of Bronze Age Europe needed." },
    { title: "One evening at Must Farm",
      text: ["Monuments tell you what people thought was worth commemorating. They are very poor at telling you what an ordinary evening looked like. For that, the best site in Britain is a fire.",
        "Around **850 BC**, in the Cambridgeshire fens, a settlement of large roundhouses built on stilts over a river channel burned down and fell straight into the water, where the mud sealed it. Excavated between 2015 and 2016, **Must Farm** produced the contents of houses that had been lived in for less than a year: wooden bowls and platters, a box of tools, glass beads, textiles of fine linen thread, and pots with meals still inside them, one of them a wheat-and-honey porridge with the spoon left in.",
        "It also produced things the monuments never do — the layout of a house, where people kept the lamb they were eating, and evidence that the whole place was abandoned in a hurry, since nobody came back for possessions worth a great deal. The site is routinely called Britain's Pompeii, which is journalism, but the principle is sound: a catastrophe that happens fast enough preserves the ordinary."],
      key: "Must Farm, burnt c. 850 BC and preserved in river mud, gives an unmatched picture of everyday Bronze Age life." },
    { title: "Fields, and the throwing away of wealth",
      text: ["The middle Bronze Age is when the British landscape starts to look like a landscape rather than a wilderness with clearings in it. On **Dartmoor**, stone boundaries called **reaves** run for miles in coordinated systems, dividing whole valleys into blocks; in Wessex and the Thames valley, ditched field systems appear on the same scale. Somebody was allocating land, and somebody had the authority to make the allocation stick.",
        "Alongside that comes a habit that has puzzled archaeologists for a century: enormous quantities of good metalwork deliberately thrown into rivers, bogs and pools, and never recovered. Not lost — placed. At **Flag Fen** near Peterborough a timber alignment nearly a kilometre long runs into the fen, and along it people deposited swords, daggers and ornaments over four hundred years, many of them broken or bent first so they could not be used again.",
        "From about **1200 BC** the system starts to come apart. Pollen and peat cores show a wetter, cooler climate; upland field systems are abandoned; and hoards of bronze grow larger and commoner right at the point when bronze stops being scarce. Then iron arrives.",
        "The usual reading is that this was an economic revolution disguised as a technological one, and it is an **Interpretation** worth stating as such. Bronze required long-distance trade in two rare metals, so whoever controlled the routes controlled the wealth. Iron ore is almost everywhere in Britain, and a competent local smith can work it. A metal that anyone can obtain is not much use as a way of keeping other people beneath you."],
      key: "Field systems and mass deliberate deposition mark the Bronze Age; iron's arrival broke the elite control that scarce bronze allowed." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "Why was Britain important to the Bronze Age world?", options: ["It exported grain", "It had both copper and tin, which rarely occur together", "It controlled the Rhine", "It produced iron first"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Bronze needs both metals, and most regions had to import at least one of them." },
    { q: "What makes Must Farm so valuable?", options: ["It is the oldest site in Britain", "It burned and sank quickly, preserving ordinary household life", "It contains royal burials", "It has written records"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Monuments record what was commemorated; a fast catastrophe records what was ordinary." },
  ],
},

{
  id: "brit-hillforts", set: "ancient-britain", act: "Before Writing", title: "Hillforts, Chariots and the Iron Age", era: "c. 800 – 100 BC", minutes: 7,
  intro: "The society Rome would eventually meet: no cities, no writing, no coinage for most of it, and some of the finest metalwork in Europe.",
  beats: [
    { title: "Living in the round",
      text: ["For roughly a thousand years, almost everyone in Britain lived in a **roundhouse**: a circle of posts, wattle and daub walls, a conical thatched roof, a hearth in the middle, and no chimney — the smoke filtered through the thatch and kept the roof dry and the insects down.",
        "One detail recurs so consistently that it cannot be accident. Across Britain, roundhouse doorways face east or south-east far more often than chance allows. The practical explanation is that this puts the door away from the prevailing south-westerly weather and catches the morning light. The other explanation is that it faces the midwinter sunrise, and that a house was oriented the way a monument was. Both are plausible, the evidence does not separate them, and it is filed here as an **Interpretation** rather than a finding.",
        "What is not in doubt is the farming. Iron Age Britain grew spelt and emmer wheat, barley, beans and flax, kept cattle, sheep and pigs, and stored grain in underground pits sealed with clay — which works, because the grain at the edges germinates, uses up the oxygen, and preserves the rest. A pit could hold a tonne and keep it for a year."],
      key: "Iron Age Britons lived in roundhouses with doorways facing east or south-east and stored grain in sealed underground pits." },
    { title: "What were the hillforts for?",
      text: ["There are over three thousand hillforts in Britain, and they have been argued about for a century. **Maiden Castle** in Dorset is the largest, with multiple ramparts enclosing forty-seven acres and an entrance deliberately built as a maze; **Danebury** in Hampshire was excavated over twenty years and produced tens of thousands of finds.",
        "The obvious reading is defensive, and some of the evidence supports it: the ramparts are enormous, the entrances are designed to make attackers turn, and slingstones have been found in caches by the gates in tens of thousands. But most hillforts show little sign of ever being attacked, many were not permanently occupied, and a good number are on ground that is not defensible at all.",
        "Danebury complicates it further. Inside were rows of granaries and hundreds of storage pits — far more than the people living there could need. That points at a central place where a district's surplus was gathered, which is an economic and political function rather than a military one. Some of those pits, after they stopped holding grain, received human remains, sometimes complete bodies and sometimes selected parts.",
        "The current position is that a hillfort was not one kind of thing. Refuge, granary, meeting place, market, statement — different sites at different times, and the word we use flattens all of it into a fort because that is what antiquarians assumed they were looking at."],
      key: "Hillforts served as refuges, granaries, central places and displays; treating them all as forts is an antiquarian assumption." },
    { title: "The art, and the violence",
      text: ["Iron Age Britain produced no literature and some of the best metalwork in Europe. The **Battersea Shield** and the **Waterloo Helmet** — the only horned helmet from Iron Age Europe — came out of the Thames in the nineteenth century, both in the flowing curvilinear style called **La Tène**, both too fine and too thin to have been meant for a battlefield. The **Snettisham** hoards in Norfolk produced gold and silver torcs by the dozen, including the Great Torc, buried in a field in a series of separate pits.",
        "In East Yorkshire, a group known to archaeologists as the **Arras culture** buried some of its dead with dismantled two-wheeled chariots, occasionally with the ponies. At Pocklington in 2018 one was found with the horses upright in the grave as if pulling.",
        "And in a Cheshire peat bog in 1984, a man was found who had died around the first century AD, in his mid-twenties, well fed and with manicured nails. **Lindow Man** was struck twice on the head, garrotted, and had his throat cut, then went face-down into a pool. The triple death has been read as ritual sacrifice, and that reading is the popular one; it has also been read as an execution or a murder. It remains **Contested**, and the confident sacrificial version usually arrives with druids attached to it that the evidence does not supply.",
        "There is a trap in all of this worth naming before Rome arrives. The objects that survive best are the ones deliberately put into water and left there — weapons, shields, torcs. That is a religious practice, not a casualty list. A society can look extremely warlike in the archaeological record simply because it kept throwing its finest weapons into rivers."],
      key: "La Tène metalwork, chariot burials and bog bodies survive largely through deliberate deposition, which can make a society look more warlike than it was." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "What does Danebury's storage capacity suggest?", options: ["A large permanent population", "A central place gathering a district's surplus", "A Roman supply depot", "Famine"], correct: 1,
      explain: "There were far more granaries and pits than the resident population needed, pointing at an economic role." },
    { q: "Why can Iron Age Britain look unusually warlike in the archaeology?", options: ["Written accounts of constant war", "Fine weapons survive because they were deliberately deposited in water", "Mass graves at every hillfort", "Roman census records"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Deliberate deposition is a religious practice; it preserves weapons regardless of how often they were used." },
  ],
},
);

CHAPTERS.push(
{
  id: "brit-pytheas", set: "ancient-britain", act: "The First Accounts", title: "The First Written Britain", era: "c. 450 – 325 BC", minutes: 8,
  intro: "The earliest description of Britain survives only in the words of men quoting it in order to call it a lie.",
  beats: [
    { title: "Herodotus declines to make something up",
      text: ["The first mention of Britain in surviving literature is a refusal. Writing in the fifth century BC, **Herodotus** discusses the **Cassiterides** — the Tin Islands, somewhere out beyond the known west, from which tin reached the Mediterranean. And then he stops. He says he cannot speak about them with any certainty, that he has not been able to find anyone who has seen this sea for himself, and that despite trying he has no first-hand account.",
        "It is a small passage and it is worth dwelling on, because it is the opposite of what usually happened to the edges of the map. Herodotus had a reputation for credulity in antiquity and has been mocked for it since; here he had a marketable mystery, no witnesses, and he wrote down that he did not know.",
        "So Britain enters the written record as an absence — a place from which a valuable metal arrived, whose location the people receiving it either did not know or would not say. The tin traders of Gades and Carthage had no commercial interest in publishing their route, and the Cassiterides remain unidentified: Cornwall, Brittany, the Scillies and the north-west Iberian coast have all been argued for, and the question is **Unknown**."],
      key: "Herodotus mentions the Tin Islands and explicitly says he has no reliable information about them." },
    { title: "Pytheas sails north",
      text: ["Around **325 BC** a Greek from the colony of **Massalia** — Marseille — went to find out. **Pytheas** sailed out past the Pillars of Heracles, up the Atlantic coast, and around Britain, and wrote a book about it called *On the Ocean*.",
        "The book is lost. Every word of Pytheas that survives comes to us inside the work of somebody else, and most of those people quote him in order to say he was lying. **Strabo** calls him an arch-falsifier and says he deceived people about everything. **Polybius**, normally the soberest historian in the ancient world, does not believe a private individual of modest means could have made such a voyage. **Pliny the Elder** and others quote him more neutrally, and Hipparchus the astronomer used his latitude measurements as data.",
        "Consider what he was disbelieved for. He said the northern seas had days so long that the sun barely set, and nights that were correspondingly short. He said that beyond Britain, six days' sail north, lay a land he called **Thule**, and that near it the sea, the land and the air merged into something that was none of them and could neither be walked on nor sailed through — which is a reasonable description of pack ice and freezing fog by a man who had no word for either. He said the tides were governed by the moon.",
        "Every one of those is right. The man his contemporaries called a fantasist was reporting the Arctic accurately to people who had no framework for it, and the framework is what they lacked, not the honesty."],
      key: "Pytheas of Massalia sailed round Britain c. 325 BC; his lost book survives only in quotations by writers who disbelieved him." },
    { title: "What Britain looked like from outside",
      text: ["What can be reassembled from the fragments is the first outside description of this island, and it is recognisable. Pytheas gives the name — some form of **Prettanikē**, from which Britain comes, and which appears to be what the inhabitants called themselves rather than a Greek coinage. He describes the shape as a triangle and gives the coastline a length; the figure is too large but the proportions are not absurd.",
        "He measured his latitudes with a **gnomon**, a vertical rod whose midday shadow gives you your position north or south, and the readings that survive are close enough to be checked. That is not the method of a man inventing a voyage from a library in Marseille.",
        "Diodorus, drawing on Pytheas at second or third hand, describes the tin working at **Belerion** — the Cornish peninsula — where the ore was smelted into knuckle-bone shapes and carried at low tide to an island called **Ictis**, and from there shipped to Gaul and overland to the Mediterranean. St Michael's Mount is the usual candidate for Ictis and is **Probable** rather than proven.",
        "And there is one domestic detail that reads as true because nobody would bother to invent it. The Britons, he says, thresh their grain indoors in large buildings, because there is so little sunshine and so much rain that an open threshing floor is useless. They make a drink from grain and honey.",
        "The first written account of this country is therefore an honest one, produced by a man who went and looked, and it survived by accident inside the works of people who thought he was making it up. That is the position the whole Set is written from: the record of Britain is not what Britons said about themselves, because they said nothing that lasted. It is what outsiders wrote, filtered through whether other outsiders believed them."],
      key: "Pytheas recorded the name Prettanike, measured latitudes with a gnomon, and described the Cornish tin trade and indoor threshing." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "Why is Pytheas' account of Britain so hard to use?", options: ["It was written in a lost language", "His book is lost and survives only in quotations, mostly hostile", "He never left Marseille", "It was destroyed at Alexandria"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Strabo and Polybius quote him in order to contradict him, which is why the fragments survive at all." },
    { q: "What did Pytheas report that later proved correct?", options: ["Nothing", "The midnight sun, pack ice, and the moon governing the tides", "The location of Rome", "That Britain was joined to Gaul"], correct: 1,
      explain: "He was disbelieved because his readers had no framework for the Arctic, not because he was inaccurate." },
  ],
},

{
  id: "brit-druids", set: "ancient-britain", act: "The First Accounts", title: "The Druids, and What Can Be Known", era: "c. 300 BC – AD 61", minutes: 7,
  intro: "A real institution about which almost nothing survives, buried beneath an imaginary one about which a great deal has been written.",
  beats: [
    { title: "The evidence, all of it",
      text: ["Everything ancient that is known about druids would fit on a few pages, and almost all of it comes from a handful of Greek and Roman writers, none of whom claims to have been taught by one.",
        "The fullest account is **Caesar's**. In the sixth book of the *Gallic War* he describes a class in Gaul that handles sacrifice, ruling on disputes and the education of the young, exempt from taxes and from military service. He says they will not commit their doctrine to writing, though they use Greek letters for other business, so that it cannot escape into common circulation and so that memorising it sharpens the memory — and that a student might spend twenty years on it. He says they hold that souls do not perish but pass to other bodies. And he says the discipline was thought to have been found in **Britain** and to have been carried from there into Gaul, so that students still crossed to Britain to study it.",
        "That last claim is the reason Britain matters to the subject at all, and it should be held carefully. Caesar never met a British druid. He is reporting what he was told in Gaul about a place he had not yet invaded, in a book whose purpose was to explain to Rome why he needed to keep campaigning."],
      key: "Caesar is the fullest ancient source: an oral priestly class, twenty years of training, and a doctrine believed to originate in Britain." },
    { title: "The wicker man, and where it comes from",
      text: ["Caesar also gives the image everyone knows: enormous figures woven from branches, filled with living people and set alight, with criminals preferred but the innocent used when supply ran short. **Strabo** and **Diodorus** describe similar practices, and **Tacitus**, writing of the Roman assault on **Anglesey** in AD 60, describes groves sacred to savage rites and altars soaked with the blood of prisoners.",
        "Four sources sounds like strong evidence until you look at where they come from. Strabo, Diodorus and probably Caesar are all drawing, directly or at one remove, on a lost work by the Greek philosopher **Posidonius**, who travelled in Gaul around 100 BC. Four accounts descending from one account are one account. And every writer using it had the same practical interest: Rome conquered Gaul and Britain, and a religion that burned people alive is a conquest that explains itself.",
        "Against that, human sacrifice in Iron Age north-west Europe is not implausible on the archaeology, and bog bodies like Lindow Man are consistent with it without demonstrating it. The app classifies druidic human sacrifice as **Contested**: attested, from a compromised and largely single source, and neither safely accepted nor safely dismissed.",
        "What Rome did about it is not contested. The emperors suppressed the druids by law — Augustus barring Roman citizens, Tiberius and Claudius going further — and Suetonius Paulinus destroyed the sanctuaries on Anglesey. An empire that tolerated almost every god it met made an exception for this one, which tells you the objection was not really theological."],
      key: "The human-sacrifice accounts descend largely from one lost source, Posidonius, and served the people conquering Gaul and Britain." },
    { title: "Everything else was invented later",
      text: ["No ancient writer connects druids to **Stonehenge**, or to any stone circle. The monument was already two thousand years old when Caesar wrote, and the association is a guess made by **John Aubrey** in the seventeenth century and popularised by **William Stukeley** in the eighteenth, at a point when nobody could yet date anything and a mysterious monument needed a mysterious people to explain it.",
        "The white robes and golden sickle come from a single paragraph of **Pliny the Elder** about cutting mistletoe from an oak, which he reports as a curiosity. The rest — the orders, the ceremonies, the bardic titles, the alphabets — is modern. The Ancient Order of Druids was founded in a London tavern in 1781; the Gorsedd of the Bards was invented in 1792 by **Iolo Morganwg**, a Welsh stonemason and laudanum addict of real literary talent who also forged a large body of medieval Welsh manuscripts convincingly enough to mislead scholarship for a century.",
        "None of that makes modern druidry fraudulent as a practice; it makes it modern. The distinction this app cares about is between what can be shown and what has been supplied.",
        "So the honest summary is uncomfortable. There was a genuine, powerful and organised priestly class in Iron Age Gaul and Britain, which Rome thought dangerous enough to legislate against, and which refused on principle to write anything down. Because of that principle, we have not one word from a druid. Everything above comes from their enemies, and everything most people picture comes from the eighteenth century."],
      key: "Stonehenge, white robes and bardic orders are antiquarian and modern inventions; no druid text survives because they refused to write." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "Why do four ancient accounts of druidic sacrifice count for less than they appear to?", options: ["They are all forgeries", "They largely descend from one lost source, Posidonius, and served the conquerors", "They were written in the Middle Ages", "They contradict each other completely"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Sources sharing an origin are not independent, and every writer using it had an interest in justifying conquest." },
    { q: "Why is there no druid account of druidry?", options: ["It was destroyed by Rome", "They refused on principle to write their doctrine down", "They were illiterate", "It survives only in Welsh"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Caesar says they used Greek letters for ordinary business but would not commit doctrine to writing." },
  ],
},
);

CHAPTERS.push(
{
  id: "brit-tribes", set: "ancient-britain", act: "The Tribal Kingdoms", title: "The Kingdoms Rome Found", era: "c. 150 BC – AD 43", minutes: 7,
  intro: "By the time Rome arrived the south-east had kings, mints and Latin lettering. The wilderness in the Roman account is a literary device.",
  beats: [
    { title: "Every tribal name we use is a Roman one",
      text: ["The map in the front of every book on Iron Age Britain is a patchwork of named territories: **Catuvellauni** in Hertfordshire, **Trinovantes** in Essex, **Iceni** in Norfolk, **Brigantes** across the north, **Atrebates** in the south, **Durotriges** in Dorset, **Silures** and **Ordovices** in Wales, **Dumnonii** in the south-west.",
        "Not one of those names is recorded by the people it describes. They come from Roman writers, from **Ptolemy's** *Geography* — compiled in Alexandria a century after the conquest — and from the legends stamped on coins by a handful of rulers. The map is drawn from the paperwork of an administration that needed the island divided into governable units, and then projected backwards onto the centuries before that administration existed.",
        "What the archaeology shows is less tidy: regional differences in pottery, burial and coin use that sometimes match the Roman names and sometimes cut straight across them. Whether Iron Age Britons thought of themselves as Iceni in the way the Roman map implies, or whether identity ran at the level of a valley and a lord, is an **Interpretation** and an unresolved one. The names are used here because there is nothing else to use, not because they are known to be right."],
      key: "Tribal names come from Roman sources and coin legends; the tidy tribal map is an administrative convenience projected backwards." },
    { title: "Coins, and the first British names in writing",
      text: ["Coinage reaches Britain about 150 BC as imports from Gaul, and the design has a pedigree. The ultimate ancestor is the gold **stater of Philip II of Macedon** — a laurelled head of Apollo on one side, a two-horse chariot on the other — copied in Gaul, copied again from the copies, and carried across the Channel.",
        "What happens to it then is one of the most striking things in British art. Each generation of die-cutters worked from the previous coin rather than from any original, and the image slowly dissolves. Apollo's laurel wreath detaches and becomes a pattern of leaves and pellets floating free; the chariot loses its box, then its driver; the horse breaks into a line of curves and crescents that reads as a horse only if you already know what you are looking at. It is not incompetence — the execution is superb. It is a culture taking a naturalistic Mediterranean image and pulling it towards the abstract curvilinear style it already preferred.",
        "Then, from around **30 BC**, something new: legends. **TASCIOVANVS**. **CVNOBELINVS**. **VERICA**. **ADDEDOMAROS**. **EPPILLVS**, who put **REX** after his name. These are the first personal names of people living in Britain that we can read, and they are written by Britons — in Latin letters, on Roman-style coinage, decades before a legion came to stay. Some carry mint marks: CAMV for Camulodunum, VER for Verlamion.",
        "A society that had refused writing for a thousand years began using it at precisely the point it started dealing with Rome, and the first thing it wrote down was who was in charge."],
      key: "British coinage descends from Philip II's stater and abstracts over generations; inscribed coins from c. 30 BC give the first readable British names." },
    { title: "Inside the Roman economy before the invasion",
      text: ["**Strabo**, writing under Augustus, lists what Britain sent out and what came back. Out: grain, cattle, gold, silver, iron, hides, slaves and hunting dogs. In: ivory bracelets, necklaces, amber, glass vessels and what he calls small wares. He adds that the tribute from customs duties was already worth more than a conquest would be, once you deducted the cost of an occupying army — an argument that Britain was not worth invading, published a generation before Rome invaded it.",
        "The archaeology bears the trade out. Elite graves in the south-east from the first century BC contain Italian wine amphorae in quantity, Roman silver cups, bronze jugs and strainers — a full drinking service, buried with people who wanted to be seen consuming wine the Roman way. The **Lexden** tumulus outside Colchester held a chain-mail shirt, silver-studded furniture and a silver medallion of **Augustus** set as a pendant.",
        "Augustus himself claimed Britain rhetorically. The **Res Gestae**, his own account of his achievements carved for public display, lists among the kings who came to him as suppliants two Britons, Dubnovellaunus and Tincomarus — men who had lost power at home and turned up in Rome looking for backing, presented as evidence that the world's edges bent towards him.",
        "So the picture to hold when the legions land is not one of contact between an empire and a wilderness. The south-east of Britain had been trading with Rome for a century, using Roman-influenced coin, drinking Italian wine, and sending exiled kings to plead in Roman courts. What Rome annexed in AD 43 was a client zone it already knew well. The barbarian framing came afterwards, and it made the conquest sound larger."],
      key: "Strabo lists Britain's exports and imports; wine services and an Augustus medallion in elite graves show the south-east was inside the Roman economy before AD 43." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "Where do the Iron Age tribal names come from?", options: ["British inscriptions", "Roman writers, Ptolemy's Geography and coin legends", "Bede", "Oral tradition recorded in Wales"], correct: 1,
      explain: "The map is drawn from the records of an administration that needed governable units, then projected backwards." },
    { q: "What happened to Mediterranean coin designs once copied in Britain?", options: ["They were copied exactly", "They abstracted over generations into curvilinear pattern", "They were replaced with runes", "They stopped being used"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Each die-cutter worked from the previous coin, and the style pulled towards the abstract art Britain already preferred." },
  ],
},

{
  id: "brit-oppida", set: "ancient-britain", act: "The Tribal Kingdoms", title: "Oppida, and Kings With Mints", era: "c. 100 BC – AD 43", minutes: 7,
  intro: "In the last century before the conquest, Britain grew something that was not a hillfort and not quite a town.",
  beats: [
    { title: "Not a hillfort",
      text: ["Hillforts sit on hills, are compact, and are enclosed by a ring of rampart. The settlements that matter in the last hundred years before the conquest do none of those things. They lie low, near rivers and estuaries, they sprawl across many square miles, and instead of a circuit they are defined by long straight **dykes** — banks and ditches running for miles across the landscape, blocking approaches rather than enclosing a space. Archaeologists borrow Caesar's word for the large Gallic settlements and call them **oppida**.",
        "**Camulodunum**, at Colchester, is the largest. Its dyke system runs for something like twelve miles, defining a territory between two rivers that contained farmland, burial grounds, a religious site at Gosbecks and a mint. **Verlamion**, under St Albans, is the same idea in Hertfordshire. Beneath Roman **Silchester** excavation has found a planned street grid laid out before the conquest, with imported wine and olive oil already arriving.",
        "The word oppidum is doing a lot of work and it conceals real variety. What these places share is scale, a location chosen for access rather than defence, and evidence of concentrated wealth. Whether that makes them towns depends on a definition, and the argument is not settled."],
      key: "Oppida are sprawling low-lying complexes defined by dyke systems, not compact defended hillforts." },
    { title: "Cunobelinus",
      text: ["From about **AD 9** until roughly **AD 40**, one man ruled from Camulodunum over a territory that had swallowed the Trinovantes and much of the south-east. His coins say **CVNOBELINVS**, and they name his father: **TASCIOVANVS**. Suetonius, writing a century later, calls him *Britannorum rex* — king of the Britons — which is a Roman simplification of something more like a paramount kingship over other kings.",
        "His coinage is worth looking at. Some issues carry an ear of barley on one side and a vine leaf on the other, which reads as a statement about what the kingdom produced and what it imported. Others carry a horseman, a bull, a sphinx, Pegasus. The workmanship is Roman-influenced and the volume is large: this is a kingdom with a treasury, a mint and a message.",
        "For thirty years he kept Rome at arm's length by being useful to it. Trade flowed, exiles were tolerated, and nobody had to invade anybody. Shakespeare knew him as Cymbeline, which is the only reason most people have heard the name.",
        "Then he died, around AD 40, and his sons **Togodumnus** and **Caratacus** were less careful. They pushed against neighbours who were Roman clients, and drove out **Verica** of the Atrebates, who did what British losers had been doing for fifty years and went to Rome to complain. This time the emperor wanted a war."],
      key: "Cunobelinus ruled from Camulodunum for around thirty years with a mint and a treasury; his sons' expansion drove Verica to appeal to Rome." },
    { title: "What was actually annexed",
      text: ["It is worth being precise about what Rome invaded, because the standard picture gets it wrong in a particular direction.",
        "In the south and east of Britain in AD 42 there were rulers who minted inscribed coinage, kept treasuries, controlled territories with defined boundaries, imported Mediterranean goods at volume, conducted diplomacy with the empire, and in at least one case put the Latin word for king on their money. There were settlements of urban scale with planned streets. There was a religious geography with major sanctuaries. What there was not was writing used for anything except coins, stone architecture, or a state that outlived the man at the top of it.",
        "Beyond that zone — the west, the north, the uplands — the picture is genuinely different: no coinage, smaller settlements, and societies Rome would spend the next century failing to hold. The island was not one thing, and the conquest went easily in the part that was already halfway Roman and badly everywhere else.",
        "The barbarian Britain of the Roman sources — painted, chariot-borne, living in woods — is not invented, but it is selected. A conquest of a place with kings and mints is an annexation. A conquest of savages is a triumph, and a triumph is what Claudius needed."],
      key: "Rome annexed a zone that already had kings, mints, planned settlements and Mediterranean trade; the west and north were genuinely different." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "What distinguishes an oppidum from a hillfort?", options: ["It is on higher ground", "It sprawls in low-lying country and is defined by dyke systems", "It has stone walls", "It is Roman-built"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Oppida chose access over defence and were bounded by miles of linear earthwork rather than a circuit." },
    { q: "Why did Rome have a pretext to invade in AD 43?", options: ["A British raid on Gaul", "Verica of the Atrebates was expelled and appealed to Rome", "The druids declared war", "A trade embargo"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Cunobelinus' sons pushed against Roman clients, and an exiled king asking for restoration was a usable legal reason." },
  ],
},
);

CHAPTERS.push(
{
  id: "brit-caesar", set: "ancient-britain", act: "Rome Arrives", title: "Caesar Comes Twice", era: "55 – 54 BC", minutes: 8,
  intro: "Two invasions, no conquest, and the first name of a Briton in history — written down by the man invading him.",
  beats: [
    { title: "55 BC: a look, and a bad tide",
      text: ["Caesar's stated reason for crossing was that the Britons had been sending help to the Gauls he was fighting. It was late in the campaigning season, he took two legions and no cavalry worth the name, and the whole thing reads as reconnaissance with a political purpose attached.",
        "The landing was opposed. The transports could not beach in shallow water, the legionaries had to go over the side in armour into surf while being shot at, and by Caesar's own account they would not move until the standard-bearer of the Tenth jumped in alone and shamed them into following. It is a good story, and it is the sort of good story a commander includes when the campaign has not otherwise produced much.",
        "What produced even less was the weather. The cavalry transports, sailing separately, were driven back across the Channel by a gale. The same storm caught the beached fleet on a full-moon spring tide that Caesar says his men did not know about, wrecking or damaging a great many ships and stranding the army in a hostile country with no way home until they were repaired.",
        "He got the ships mended, fought off an attack, took hostages he mostly never received, and left before the equinox. In Rome the Senate voted twenty days of public thanksgiving. The gap between those two sentences is the point."],
      key: "The 55 BC expedition was a two-legion reconnaissance nearly wrecked by a spring tide; Rome voted twenty days of thanksgiving anyway." },
    { title: "54 BC: Cassivellaunus",
      text: ["The following year he came back properly: five legions, two thousand cavalry, and something like eight hundred ships, a fleet so large that Caesar says the Britons watching from the cliffs withdrew inland rather than contest the landing.",
        "The British response was to appoint a single commander over the usual rivalries — **Cassivellaunus**, whose territory lay north of the Thames. His name is the first name of an inhabitant of this island that we can read, and it comes to us in the war memoir of the man attacking him.",
        "He fought the campaign correctly, which is to say he refused to fight the campaign Caesar wanted. After one costly attempt at a pitched battle he disbanded most of his infantry, kept four thousand chariots, and shadowed the Roman column — driving cattle and people off the line of march, ambushing foragers, striking at the flanks and vanishing before the legions could form.",
        "Caesar's description of British chariot fighting is unusually admiring for a man describing enemies. They drive along the line hurling javelins, he writes, and the noise of the wheels and the horses is itself a weapon; then the fighter dismounts and fights on foot while the driver withdraws a little and waits, so that a retreat is always available. He notes that they train at it constantly — running out along the pole and back at a gallop — and that they combine the mobility of cavalry with the steadiness of infantry.",
        "The Romans forced the Thames at a ford Caesar says was staked below the waterline. Then the alliance did what British alliances did: the **Trinovantes**, whose king had been killed by Cassivellaunus and whose heir **Mandubracius** was in Caesar's camp, came over to Rome, and five other peoples followed. They told him where Cassivellaunus' stronghold was. It was taken. A coordinated attack by four Kentish kings on the Roman naval camp failed, and Cassivellaunus asked for terms."],
      key: "Cassivellaunus led a guerrilla campaign with four thousand chariots until the Trinovantes defected and revealed his stronghold." },
    { title: "What it was worth",
      text: ["The terms were hostages, an annual tribute, and an undertaking not to attack Mandubracius. Caesar then sailed for Gaul, where a revolt was brewing, and never came back.",
        "The tribute is never mentioned again by anyone. No garrison was left, no territory was taken, no Roman official stayed. Within a few decades British kings were minting their own coins in the south-east with no reference to Rome except stylistic.",
        "**Cicero** provides the unglamorous view, because his brother Quintus was on the expedition and wrote home. In one letter Cicero passes on the news that it is now established there is not a scrap of silver in the island and no prospect of booty except slaves — and adds, drily, that one should not expect any of them to be educated. This is the private opinion of the Roman political class while the public thanksgivings were being voted.",
        "A century and a half later **Tacitus** delivered the verdict that stuck: Caesar did not hand Britain over to Rome, he merely showed it to his successors. It is a fair summary, and it also flatters Caesar, since the alternative summary is that he invaded the same island twice and came away with hostages and a story.",
        "For Britain the lasting consequence was not military. It was that the island now existed in Roman minds as unfinished business — a place a divine Julius had been to, which a later emperor might therefore complete."],
      key: "No garrison, no territory and a tribute never heard of again; Tacitus judged that Caesar showed Britain to his successors rather than conquering it." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "How did Cassivellaunus fight the 54 BC campaign?", options: ["A single pitched battle", "Guerrilla warfare with chariots, refusing the battle Caesar wanted", "Naval raids on Gaul", "He surrendered immediately"], correct: 1,
      explain: "He disbanded most of his infantry, kept four thousand chariots and struck at the column's flanks and foragers." },
    { q: "What did Caesar's expeditions leave behind in Britain?", options: ["A province", "A legion at Camulodunum", "No garrison, no territory and a tribute never mentioned again", "A road network"], correct: 2,
      explain: "The lasting effect was that Britain became unfinished business in Roman politics, not a possession." },
  ],
},

{
  id: "brit-claudius", set: "ancient-britain", act: "Rome Arrives", title: "The Conquest of AD 43", era: "AD 43 – 47", minutes: 7,
  intro: "An emperor who badly needed a war, an exiled king who provided the excuse, and a province ruled at first mostly by Britons.",
  beats: [
    { title: "An emperor who needed a triumph",
      text: ["**Claudius** came to the throne in AD 41 because the Praetorian Guard found him behind a curtain after they had murdered his nephew. He was middle-aged, scholarly, walked with a limp, and had never commanded anything. In a system where an emperor's standing rested on military glory, that was a structural weakness, and everybody knew it.",
        "Britain was the obvious answer. It was unfinished business left by the deified Julius, it was across the Ocean — which had propaganda value in itself, since the Ocean was the edge of the world — and it was not, on any sober assessment, very dangerous.",
        "**Caligula** had already tried, in AD 40, and Suetonius says the expedition ended with the army drawn up on the Channel beach and ordered to collect seashells as spoils of the Ocean. The story is repeated everywhere and it comes from a source with a professional interest in making Caligula ridiculous; it may garble an order about siege engines or a genuine cancelled crossing. Treat it as **Contested**.",
        "The legal pretext arrived on its own. **Verica** of the Atrebates, driven out by Cunobelinus' sons, reached Rome and asked for restoration. Restoring a friendly king is a respectable reason to send four legions."],
      key: "Claudius needed military credibility; Verica's appeal supplied a legal pretext for invading Britain in AD 43." },
    { title: "The fighting",
      text: ["Four legions — the Second, Ninth, Fourteenth and Twentieth — with auxiliaries, perhaps forty thousand men, under **Aulus Plautius**. They very nearly did not go: the troops mutinied at the Channel, refusing to campaign beyond the known world, until an imperial freedman named Narcissus climbed the tribunal to address them and the army shouted him down with cries of *Io Saturnalia* — the festival where slaves and masters change places — and then, having made the joke, embarked.",
        "The decisive engagement was fought over two days at a river, generally taken to be the **Medway**, which is unusual in itself: ancient battles rarely lasted two days. Cassius Dio describes auxiliaries swimming the river in armour to hamstring the chariot horses on the far bank, and Vespasian — then a legionary commander, later emperor — crossing higher up. **Togodumnus** died shortly afterwards. His brother **Caratacus** went west.",
        "In the south-west Vespasian fought his own campaign, and Suetonius credits him with reducing twenty oppida and taking the Isle of Wight. For most of the twentieth century the star exhibit for that campaign was the so-called war cemetery at **Maiden Castle**, with a ballista bolt in one man's spine, read as the debris of a Roman storm. That reading has largely been abandoned: the burials accumulated over a period rather than in one afternoon, and the injuries are mixed. It is a good example of an assumption hardening into a fact and then dissolving again, and the honest classification of a Roman assault on Maiden Castle is now **Contested**."],
      key: "Four legions under Aulus Plautius won a two-day battle at the Medway; the Maiden Castle war cemetery is no longer read as evidence of a Roman storm." },
    { title: "Sixteen days, and then rule through Britons",
      text: ["With the fighting effectively decided, Plautius halted and sent for the emperor. Claudius crossed with reinforcements and, Dio says, elephants, spent **sixteen days** in Britain, took Camulodunum, received the submission of British kings, and went home to a triumph. A fragment of the arch raised in Rome to commemorate it records that he accepted the surrender of eleven British kings without any loss.",
        "That number is the important detail, and it is usually skipped. Rome did not, in AD 43, occupy Britain. It defeated one coalition and then governed most of the south through Britons who agreed to work with it.",
        "**Cogidubnus** ruled a large kingdom in the south, and an inscription from Chichester gives him the extraordinary style *rex magnus Britanniae* — great king in Britain — with Roman citizenship and the emperor's family name attached to his own. **Prasutagus** kept the Iceni in Norfolk. **Cartimandua** ruled the Brigantes across the north, the largest territory of all, and held it as a Roman ally for a quarter of a century.",
        "Client kingship is cheap, and it works until the client dies. Every one of those three arrangements would end badly, and two of them would end in the destruction of Roman towns. The province Rome founded in AD 43 was not a conquered country. It was a set of agreements with individuals, and agreements with individuals expire."],
      key: "Claudius spent sixteen days in Britain and took the surrender of eleven kings; Rome then governed the south through client rulers." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "Why did Claudius want to invade Britain?", options: ["To stop raids on Gaul", "He needed military credibility, and Britain was Caesar's unfinished business", "To seize silver mines", "To suppress the druids"], correct: 1,
      explain: "An emperor's standing rested on military glory, and Claudius had none." },
    { q: "How did Rome rule most of southern Britain immediately after AD 43?", options: ["Direct military occupation", "Through British client kings such as Cogidubnus, Prasutagus and Cartimandua", "Through the druids", "It withdrew entirely"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Client kingship is cheap and effective, and it lasts exactly as long as the client does." },
  ],
},

{
  id: "brit-caratacus", set: "ancient-britain", act: "Rome Arrives", title: "Caratacus", era: "AD 43 – 51", minutes: 7,
  intro: "Nine years of resistance, a betrayal that was really a treaty being kept, and a speech in Rome that a Roman wrote.",
  beats: [
    { title: "Nine years",
      text: ["After the Medway and his brother's death, **Caratacus** did not surrender and did not go home. He went west, into country Rome had not touched, and made himself the commander of other people's wars — first among the **Silures** of south-east Wales, then the **Ordovices** further north.",
        "Tacitus, who is our source for nearly all of this, says that his repeated successes and escapes made him the most famous man in Britain, and that he traded on the reputation: a leader who had survived Rome for years was worth following even by people with no reason to care about a dispossessed south-eastern king.",
        "The Silures were the hardest opponents Rome met in Britain. The governor **Ostorius Scapula** is reported to have said publicly that they would have to be exterminated or transplanted, which is the kind of remark that guarantees a long war, and Tacitus notes it hardened them. Scapula died in post, worn out, with the war unfinished."],
      key: "Caratacus fought on for nine years in Wales with the Silures and Ordovices, becoming the most famous man in Britain." },
    { title: "The last battle, and Cartimandua",
      text: ["In **AD 51** he finally accepted a set-piece battle, on ground of his own choosing in Ordovician territory — a hillside behind a river, with stone ramparts thrown up where the slope was weak. Tacitus gives him a speech to the assembled tribes about ancestors who had driven Caesar out, and describes the Roman troops hesitating at the sight of the position before the legionaries formed testudo, pulled the stones apart and went up.",
        "The site is unknown. Caer Caradoc in Shropshire and several other hills carry the tradition, and there is no evidence for any of them; the location is **Unknown**.",
        "His wife, daughter and brothers were taken. Caratacus escaped north to the **Brigantes** and threw himself on the protection of their queen, **Cartimandua**, who put him in chains and handed him to the Romans.",
        "She is remembered as a traitor, and that framing deserves examination. Cartimandua was not a British patriot who betrayed the cause; she was the ruler of the largest kingdom in Britain, holding it together as a Roman ally, and the treaty that kept Roman armies out of her territory required exactly this. Sheltering the empire's most wanted enemy would have brought four legions into Brigantia. She protected her own people at the price of one foreign fugitive, which is a recognisable calculation, and Tacitus — who disliked her for other reasons — never suggests she had a choice."],
      key: "Defeated in AD 51 at an unknown Welsh hillside, Caratacus fled to Cartimandua of the Brigantes, who handed him over under her Roman treaty." },
    { title: "The speech",
      text: ["He was sent to Rome and paraded. Tacitus describes the crowds, the family led past in chains, the whole city turned out to look at a man who had held out for nine years — and then has him address Claudius.",
        "The speech, in summary, runs: I had horses, men and arms, and did not want to lose them; if you want to rule everyone, does it follow that everyone should accept slavery? Had I surrendered at once, neither my fall nor your glory would be remembered, and my execution would be forgotten with me. Spare me, and I will be a lasting example of your mercy.",
        "Claudius pardoned him, his wife and his brothers, and they lived out their lives in Italy. Dio adds that Caratacus, looking at the scale of the city, asked how people who owned all this could possibly want his poor huts.",
        "Every word of that speech was written by **Tacitus**, fifty-odd years after the event, in the accepted manner of ancient historiography, which composed speeches to suit the speaker and the occasion rather than reporting them. Nobody at the time thought that dishonest, and nobody now should read it as transcript.",
        "But notice what it is doing. Tacitus was a Roman senator, writing under emperors he distrusted, and he repeatedly puts his sharpest criticisms of Roman conquest into the mouths of the conquered. The most quoted British statement of the entire ancient period is a Roman aristocrat's composition, and it is an argument against Rome. That is not a British voice surviving. It is the absence of one, decorated."],
      key: "Caratacus was paraded in Rome and pardoned; the famous speech was composed by Tacitus and is a Roman argument against Roman conquest." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "Why did Cartimandua hand Caratacus to Rome?", options: ["Personal enmity", "Her treaty as a Roman client required it, and sheltering him meant war in Brigantia", "She was bribed by Claudius", "He had killed her husband"], correct: 1,
      explain: "She was protecting the largest kingdom in Britain at the price of one foreign fugitive." },
    { q: "How should Caratacus' speech in Rome be treated?", options: ["A verbatim record", "Composed by Tacitus in the manner of ancient historiography", "Written by Caratacus himself", "A medieval forgery"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Ancient historians composed speeches to suit speaker and occasion; Tacitus used the conquered to criticise Rome." },
  ],
},
);

CHAPTERS.push(
{
  id: "brit-boudica", set: "ancient-britain", act: "The Province", title: "Boudica", era: "AD 60 – 61", minutes: 9,
  intro: "A client king's will was ignored, three cities burned, and the woman who did it spent thirteen centuries forgotten before England needed her.",
  beats: [
    { title: "The will",
      text: ["**Prasutagus** of the **Iceni** had ruled Norfolk as a Roman ally since the conquest, and he made the arrangement client kings made: a will leaving his kingdom jointly to the emperor and to his two daughters, on the assumption that naming Nero as co-heir would protect the rest.",
        "Rome read it differently. A client kingdom was a personal arrangement with a man, and when the man died the arrangement died. The procurator's staff moved in to take an inheritance, and Tacitus describes what followed in the flattest possible sentences: the kingdom was plundered by centurions, the household by slaves, the chief men stripped of their ancestral estates, the king's relatives treated as property. **Boudica** was flogged. Her daughters were raped.",
        "There was a financial trigger as well. Cassius Dio says that loans forced on the British aristocracy — including forty million sesterces lent by the philosopher **Seneca** — were called in suddenly and in full, and that the procurator **Catus Decianus** was simultaneously demanding the return of money that Claudius had given as grants and was now reclassifying as debt.",
        "And there was a symbol. At **Camulodunum** the veterans' colony had built a temple to the deified Claudius, and Tacitus reports that the Britons were paying for it — a shrine to the god who had conquered them, funded by them, staffed by priests chosen from among them, which he says appeared as a citadel of everlasting tyranny."],
      key: "Prasutagus' will was ignored, the Iceni estates were plundered, Boudica was flogged and her daughters raped." },
    { title: "Three cities",
      text: ["The rising began among the Iceni and the **Trinovantes** joined it, which mattered, because the Trinovantes had lost their land to the veterans at Camulodunum.",
        "**Camulodunum** had no walls. It had been a legionary fortress, the defences had been levelled when it became a colony, and the veterans had assumed that being Romans would be enough. Tacitus reports the omens beforehand — the statue of Victory falling face-down as if fleeing, wailing heard in the theatre, the sea running the colour of blood — and then the sober facts: the town taken quickly, the survivors holding the temple for two days, and the relief column of the **Ninth Legion** ambushed on the road with its infantry destroyed.",
        "**Suetonius Paulinus**, the governor, reached **Londinium** ahead of the rebels with a small force, looked at it, and made the decision a professional makes. The town was not defensible, he did not have an army, and holding it meant losing the province. Tacitus says the inhabitants begged him to stay, and that he gave the signal to march and took with him only those who could keep up. Londinium burned, and so, next, did **Verulamium**.",
        "Tacitus puts the dead at seventy thousand Romans and provincials. The number is a Roman estimate of a catastrophe and should be held loosely. What is not loose is the physical evidence: in all three cities excavation reaches a layer of burnt daub, ash and melted glass, red where the clay was fired, sitting between the pre-revolt town and everything after — the **Boudican destruction horizon**, and one of the few places where an event in Tacitus can be picked up in a trench."],
      key: "Camulodunum, Londinium and Verulamium were destroyed; the red destruction layer in all three confirms Tacitus." },
    { title: "Where the governor was",
      text: ["The revolt's timing was not chance. Paulinus was at the other end of the country, on **Ynys Môn** — Anglesey — attacking the druidic sanctuaries, which is why there was no field army between the Iceni and the coast.",
        "Tacitus' account of that assault is one of the strangest passages in Roman literature: the troops drawn up on the shore, the enemy line dense with weapons, and running between the ranks women in black funeral dress with dishevelled hair, carrying torches, while the druids raised their hands and called down curses. The soldiers, he says, stood still as if their limbs were paralysed, until their commander shamed them into advancing. Then the groves were cut down.",
        "It is written to be read as a victory of Roman discipline over superstition, and it also records that Rome went out of its way to destroy a religion. Rome tolerated nearly every god it met. The exception it made here says the objection was political — a priesthood with authority across tribal boundaries is a rival network, whatever it believes."],
      key: "Paulinus was destroying the druidic sanctuaries on Anglesey when the revolt began, which is why no field army stood in its way." },
    { title: "Watling Street, and after",
      text: ["Paulinus came back down the road with about ten thousand men and chose the ground with great care: a defile with woods at his back and open country in front, so that the British numbers could not wrap around him and there was nothing behind to be attacked from. The site has never been identified and is **Unknown**, though a stretch of Watling Street in the Midlands is the usual guess.",
        "The Britons brought their families and parked the wagons in a crescent behind the battle line to watch. When the line broke, the wagons were what it broke against. Tacitus claims eighty thousand Britons dead against four hundred Romans, which is the sort of ratio that means the killing went on long after the fighting stopped.",
        "How **Boudica** died is not known. Tacitus says she took poison. Dio says she fell ill and died, and was given a rich burial. Nobody records where. The two accounts are not reconcilable and the app classifies it as **Unknown**.",
        "The aftermath was harsh enough that Rome noticed. Paulinus conducted punitive sweeps; the new procurator **Classicianus** — a Gaul, married to a woman from a Treveran family, whose tombstone is in the British Museum — reported over the governor's head that the province would never be pacified while the man who caused the war was running the peace. Paulinus was recalled. That an imperial official argued for restraint on the grounds that vengeance was bad administration is worth noticing."],
      key: "Paulinus destroyed the revolt at an unidentified defile; Boudica's death is unknown, and Classicianus got the governor recalled." },
    { title: "The thirteen hundred missing years",
      text: ["Then she disappears. **Gildas**, writing in Britain five centuries later, does not mention her. **Bede** does not. The medieval chroniclers do not. For roughly thirteen hundred years nobody in Britain knew Boudica had existed, because the only substantial account was in a manuscript of Tacitus that was not read here.",
        "She comes back with the Renaissance rediscovery of the text, and every age since has needed a different version of her. Tudor writers used her as evidence that England had always resisted foreign domination. **Holinshed** and **Spenser** made her a warrior queen for a reign that had one. The eighteenth century made her a noble savage.",
        "The Victorians completed the job, and the irony is total. **Tennyson** wrote her as a prophetess of empire. **Thomas Thornycroft's** bronze — Boudica in a scythed chariot, which is a Persian device with no evidence in Britain — was commissioned in the reign of a queen-empress and installed beside Westminster Bridge, so that a woman who destroyed three Roman towns rather than be ruled from abroad now stands guard over the parliament of the largest empire in history. Her name even helped: *Boudica* derives from a Brittonic word for victory, cognate with the Welsh *buddug*, and it is very likely a title rather than a name.",
        "This is the Set's argument in one figure. Britain produced no account of itself, so the only Boudica anyone has is the one a Roman senator wrote for Roman purposes — and into that empty space every later century has poured whatever it needed. The further from the evidence you get, the more confident the portrait becomes."],
      key: "Boudica was unknown in Britain for thirteen centuries until Tacitus was rediscovered, and was then remade by each age that needed her." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "Why did Rome ignore Prasutagus' will?", options: ["It was a forgery", "A client kingdom was a personal arrangement that died with the client", "Nero refused the inheritance", "The Iceni had already revolted"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Client kingship is cheap and works until the client dies — the same weakness that runs through the whole province." },
    { q: "What confirms Tacitus' account of the destruction?", options: ["Iceni coin hoards", "A layer of burnt daub and ash beneath all three cities", "Boudica's grave", "Inscriptions at Camulodunum"], correct: 1,
      explain: "The Boudican destruction horizon is one of the few places a Tacitean event can be picked up in a trench." },
    { q: "Why was Boudica unknown in Britain for over a thousand years?", options: ["Her name was banned", "The only substantial account was in Tacitus, not read here until the Renaissance", "Gildas suppressed her", "She was confused with Cartimandua"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Every later Boudica is a reconstruction poured into the space where a British account should be." },
  ],
},

{
  id: "brit-agricola", set: "ancient-britain", act: "The Province", title: "Agricola, and the Man Who Wrote Him", era: "AD 77 – 84", minutes: 8,
  intro: "The best-documented governor of Roman Britain is the one whose son-in-law wrote the book.",
  beats: [
    { title: "A biography by the family",
      text: ["**Gnaeus Julius Agricola** governed Britain for around seven years, which was unusually long, and is by a wide margin the best-recorded official in the province's history. That is not because he was the most important. It is because his daughter married **Tacitus**, and Tacitus wrote his life.",
        "The *Agricola* was published in AD 98, shortly after the assassination of Domitian, and it is doing several jobs at once. It is a funeral tribute to a father-in-law. It is a defence of men who served tyrannical emperors without resisting — Tacitus' own position — arguing that great men can exist under bad rulers and that ostentatious martyrdom achieves nothing. And it is an ethnographic and geographical account of Britain, which is why it is quoted in every book on the subject.",
        "None of that makes it false. It makes it a case for the defence, written by a relative, about a man whose reputation was also the author's. Where it praises Agricola's judgement, competence and restraint, remember who is doing the judging, and that the rival governors it dismisses have left nobody to write for them."],
      key: "Agricola is the best-documented governor because his son-in-law Tacitus wrote his biography as a family defence." },
    { title: "Amenities, and what they were for",
      text: ["The passage everybody quotes describes what Agricola did in the winter between campaigns. He encouraged and assisted the building of temples, public squares and proper houses; he had the sons of the chiefs educated, and preferred British ability to Gallic; and Latin, previously resisted, became something men wanted. The toga was seen everywhere. Porticoes, baths and elegant dinners followed.",
        "Then Tacitus turns the knife. All this the inexperienced called civilisation, he writes, when it was part of their enslavement.",
        "That is not a modern reading imposed on the source. It is the Roman source itself, describing his own father-in-law's policy, and identifying it as a mechanism: the conquered are given comforts that make the conquest comfortable, and having accepted them they no longer want it undone. It is one of the sharpest descriptions of soft power written before the phrase existed, and it was written by a man defending the person who applied it.",
        "The archaeology shows the policy landing. In the decades after Agricola the civitas capitals get their fora and basilicas, the bath-houses go up, and the aristocracy that had minted its own coins seventy years earlier starts commissioning inscriptions in Latin."],
      key: "Tacitus describes Agricola's building and education programme and calls it part of enslavement — an ancient account of soft power." },
    { title: "North, and Mons Graupius",
      text: ["The campaigns went further north than any before or after. Agricola took the army into **Caledonia**, built a road and a chain of forts up the eastern side, and in AD 83 or 84 brought a Caledonian confederation to battle at a place Tacitus calls **Mons Graupius**. The site is not identified; candidates run from Bennachie in Aberdeenshire to points much further south, and it is **Unknown**. The mountain range now called the Grampians is named after a misprint of Graupius in a fifteenth-century edition.",
        "Before the battle Tacitus gives the Caledonian leader **Calgacus** a speech, and it contains the most famous sentence ever written about the Roman empire: they make a desolation and call it peace. It is preceded by the claim that the Romans are robbers of the world who, having exhausted the land, now scour the sea; that they give the names of empire to plunder, slaughter and theft.",
        "Calgacus is named nowhere else and Tacitus was not present. The speech is a composition, like Caratacus', and again it is a Roman using a Briton to say what a Roman senator could not say in his own voice.",
        "The battle is reported as a Roman victory with ten thousand Britons dead against three hundred and sixty Romans. Ratios like that are a genre convention rather than a casualty return. Afterwards Agricola sent the fleet round the north of the island, which established for the first time by observation that Britain is an island — Pytheas had said so four hundred years earlier and had not been believed."],
      key: "Mons Graupius (site unknown) was won c. AD 83; Calgacus' famous speech is Tacitus' composition, and the fleet then circumnavigated Britain." },
    { title: "Conquered, and immediately let go",
      text: ["Agricola was recalled shortly afterwards. Tacitus attributes it to Domitian's jealousy of a subordinate's success, which is exactly what a defensive biography would attribute it to, and may still be true.",
        "What happened next is not in doubt. The forts north of the Forth were dismantled within a few years — at **Inchtuthil**, the legionary fortress built to hold the Highland line, the garrison left so methodically that they buried nearly a million unused iron nails rather than leave them for anyone else, and archaeologists dug them up in the 1960s. The northern gains were abandoned, and troops were pulled to the Danube where the empire had a real emergency.",
        "Tacitus' verdict on the whole enterprise, in his *Histories*, is five words in Latin and it is bitter: Britain was completely conquered and immediately let go. It is the closest thing Rome produced to an admission that it had bitten off an island it did not intend to finish, and everything that follows — the walls, the northern wars, three centuries of holding a line across the narrowest part of the country — is the consequence."],
      key: "The northern conquests were abandoned within years; Tacitus wrote that Britain was conquered and immediately let go." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "Why must the Agricola be read carefully?", options: ["It is a forgery", "It was written by Agricola's son-in-law as a family defence", "It was written 500 years later", "It survives only in fragments"], correct: 1,
      explain: "It is a case for the defence, and the rival governors it dismisses have nobody writing for them." },
    { q: "What does Tacitus say about baths, togas and porticoes in Britain?", options: ["They civilised the Britons", "The inexperienced called it civilisation when it was part of their enslavement", "They were a waste of money", "They were resisted entirely"], correct: 1,
      explain: "It is an ancient description of soft power, written by the man defending the governor who applied it." },
  ],
},
);

CHAPTERS.push(
{
  id: "brit-walls", set: "ancient-britain", act: "The Province", title: "The Wall, and the Wars Beyond It", era: "AD 122 – 211", minutes: 8,
  intro: "Rome spent three centuries holding a line across the narrowest part of an island it had declared conquered.",
  beats: [
    { title: "Seventy-three miles",
      text: ["**Hadrian** came to Britain in AD 122 and the decision was taken to build a barrier from the Tyne to the Solway: seventy-three miles of it, stone in the east, turf at first in the west, with a fortified gateway every Roman mile, two turrets between each pair, a ditch in front, and later a chain of forts astride the line itself. Behind it ran the **vallum**, a flat-bottomed ditch with banks, marking off a military zone.",
        "What it was for has been argued about since the eighteenth century. The one ancient statement is a sentence in the unreliable *Historia Augusta*: that Hadrian was the first to build a wall, eighty miles long, to separate the Romans from the barbarians. That is a purpose, but it does not explain the design.",
        "A wall meant to stop an army would not have a gate every mile. Milecastles with gateways north and south are the architecture of a customs post: they control, count and tax movement rather than forbidding it. And the Wall is not sited for defence in depth — in places it runs along a crag where the drop does the work, and in others it takes a line that a commander wanting a battlefield would not choose.",
        "The current reading is that it did several things at once — a statement of where Rome ended, a mechanism for taxing and monitoring traffic, a barrier against raiding rather than invasion, and a very large piece of employment for an army that was not campaigning. That it kept the north out is the one thing it demonstrably failed to do."],
      key: "Hadrian's Wall runs 73 miles with a gateway every mile; its design suits control and taxation of movement more than defence." },
    { title: "The letters at Vindolanda",
      text: ["Just south of the Wall, at **Vindolanda**, the ground is waterlogged and the rubbish did not rot. Excavation from 1973 onwards has produced hundreds of thin wooden leaves written in ink — the everyday paperwork and correspondence of a garrison around AD 100, and the closest thing to overheard speech that Roman Britain has left.",
        "The most famous is a party invitation. **Claudia Severa**, wife of a commander at a nearby fort, writes to **Sulpicia Lepidina** at Vindolanda inviting her to a birthday celebration and saying she will make the day more enjoyable by coming. Most of it is in a professional scribe's hand; the closing greeting — sister, dearest soul, farewell — is added in a second, less practised hand, which is Claudia Severa's own. It is the earliest known writing in Latin by a woman anywhere in the Roman world, and it is about a birthday.",
        "The rest is gloriously mundane. A strength report. A request for more beer for the troops. A letter enclosing socks, two pairs of sandals and underpants. An account of a man being beaten. And a tactical note, written by someone assessing the locals, that uses the word ***Brittunculi*** — wretched little Britons — which is not attested anywhere else and appears to be army slang.",
        "The garrison itself was rarely British and rarely Roman. Vindolanda's units were Tungrians and Batavians from the Low Countries. Housesteads later held a unit of Frisians, and the Wall's forts drew archers from Syria, cavalry from the Danube, and at Burgh-by-Sands a unit of **Aurelian Moors** from North Africa. At York a woman buried in the fourth century with an ivory bracelet and jet jewellery — the **Ivory Bangle Lady** — has been assessed from her skull and isotopes as of mixed ancestry including North African, and was buried with wealth.",
        "The frontier of Roman Britain was garrisoned by people from across the empire, and their descendants were Britons."],
      key: "The Vindolanda tablets include the earliest Latin writing by a woman; the Wall's garrisons came from across the empire." },
    { title: "The north was never taken",
      text: ["Twenty years after Hadrian's Wall was finished, **Antoninus Pius** — an emperor with no military record and the same problem Claudius had — ordered the army a hundred miles further north, and built the **Antonine Wall** across the Forth–Clyde isthmus: turf on a stone base, thirty-seven miles, with forts every two miles or so and a series of carved distance slabs that are the finest Roman sculpture found in Britain.",
        "It was held for roughly twenty years and abandoned. The army fell back to Hadrian's line, and that is where the frontier stayed.",
        "In AD 208 the emperor **Septimius Severus** came in person, with his sons and an enormous army, to settle the north permanently. Cassius Dio records the order he gave: that nobody should be spared, including the child in the womb, and quotes Homer to sharpen it. The campaigns were savage, made little progress against an enemy that would not stand and fight, and ended when Severus died at **York** in 211. His sons made terms and left, and the northern conquest was abandoned for the third and last time.",
        "That is the shape of Roman Britain, and it is not the shape people picture. Rome held roughly the southern two-thirds of one island for three and a half centuries, tried three times to take the rest, and each time walked away. Ireland was never invaded at all — Agricola is said to have thought one legion would do it, which is the kind of estimate people make about places they have not been. The province was defined by a frontier, and the frontier is the reason a tenth of the entire Roman army was stationed in a wet provincial backwater at the end of the world."],
      key: "The Antonine Wall was abandoned after twenty years and Severus' campaign ended with his death at York in 211; the north was never held." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "What does a gateway every Roman mile suggest about Hadrian's Wall?", options: ["It was unfinished", "It controlled and taxed movement rather than forbidding it", "It was purely ceremonial", "It was built to be attacked from"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Milecastles with gates north and south are the architecture of a customs post as much as a defence." },
    { q: "What is the Claudia Severa tablet?", options: ["A military strength report", "A birthday invitation, and the earliest known Latin writing by a woman", "A curse tablet", "A tax record"], correct: 1,
      explain: "The closing greeting is in her own hand, added to a scribe's letter." },
  ],
},

{
  id: "brit-life", set: "ancient-britain", act: "The Province", title: "Living in the Province", era: "AD 50 – 400", minutes: 7,
  intro: "Towns, roads, villas and gods — and, in one hot spring, the only surviving voices of ordinary Britons.",
  beats: [
    { title: "Towns and roads",
      text: ["Roman Britain acquired the apparatus of a province quickly. Four **coloniae** for veterans — Colchester, Lincoln, Gloucester and York — and around twenty **civitas capitals**, administrative towns for each tribal district, generally built where the tribe already had a centre and often laid out on a grid with a forum, a basilica, public baths and eventually walls.",
        "**Londinium** was not one of these. It appears to have begun as a trading settlement at the lowest bridgeable point of the Thames, was burnt by Boudica within twenty years of its founding, and rebuilt into the largest city in Britain and the seat of the governor — a capital that grew from commerce rather than being decreed.",
        "Underneath it all went the roads: something like ten thousand miles of engineered highway, built for the army and used by everyone, and so well aligned that several are still the line of a modern A-road. This is the least glamorous and most permanent thing Rome did here. The towns shrank and the villas fell down; the road from London to York is still the road from London to York."],
      key: "Four coloniae, around twenty civitas capitals and some ten thousand miles of road; Londinium grew from trade rather than decree." },
    { title: "The only ordinary voices",
      text: ["At **Bath**, the hot spring was already sacred to a goddess called **Sulis** when the Romans arrived, and rather than replace her they married her to Minerva and built a temple and bath complex around the source.",
        "People threw things into the spring, and among them were around a hundred and thirty sheets of lead scratched with writing: **curse tablets**, or more precisely petitions asking the goddess to act against a thief. Another substantial group came from a temple at Uley.",
        "They are almost the only surviving words of ordinary inhabitants of Roman Britain, and the striking thing is what they are about. Not war, not politics, not religion in any elevated sense. Somebody stole a cloak from the bath-house. Somebody took six silver coins. Somebody stole a pair of gloves, and the writer asks that the person responsible lose their mind and their eyes in the goddess's temple. One petition covers all possibilities by cursing the thief whether man or woman, slave or free — an unintended census of who might have been in the changing room.",
        "One tablet lists eighteen suspects by name. Several are written by people with British names in competent Latin. At least one appears not to be Latin at all and may be written in British Celtic, which would make it one of the only surviving traces of the spoken language of the province.",
        "Four thousand years of British history, and the first ordinary people we can hear are complaining about theft from a swimming pool. It is a corrective to every account of this island written by generals."],
      key: "The Bath and Uley curse tablets are the only substantial voices of ordinary Roman Britons, and are almost all about stolen property." },
    { title: "Villas, and everybody else",
      text: ["The **villa** is the image of Roman Britain — a working estate with a comfortable house at the centre, underfloor heating, painted plaster and mosaic floors. There are around a thousand known, and the great majority are in the south and east. Their peak is late: the fourth century, when the rest of the western empire was in visible difficulty, produced the largest and most lavish, at **Chedworth**, **Woodchester**, **Bignor** and **Lullingstone**.",
        "Most people did not live in one. Across large parts of the province the ordinary rural settlement remained a roundhouse in an enclosure, farming much as before, paying tax in grain and using enough Roman pottery to show they were connected to the economy without being transformed by it. Roman Britain was a thin, wealthy, highly visible layer over a countryside that changed slowly.",
        "It ran on unfree labour, which the mosaics do not depict. Slave shackles and neck-chains have been excavated in Britain, and Strabo listed slaves among the island's exports before the conquest.",
        "And it exported people the other way. Britons appear as auxiliary soldiers on the Danube, in North Africa and on the German frontier — men conscripted from a conquered province and sent to hold somebody else's border, which is exactly what Rome had done to the Batavians and Tungrians it stationed on the Wall. The empire moved its subjects around, and a Briton was as likely to end his life beside the Danube as a Syrian was to end his beside the Tyne."],
      key: "A thousand villas, peaking in the fourth century, sat over a countryside of roundhouses; Britons served as auxiliaries across the empire." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "What are the Bath curse tablets mostly about?", options: ["Military defeats", "Stolen property, especially from the baths", "Imperial politics", "Harvest failure"], correct: 1,
      explain: "They are almost the only ordinary voices from the province, and they are complaints about theft." },
    { q: "When did British villas reach their peak?", options: ["The first century", "The second century", "The fourth century, as the western empire faltered elsewhere", "After Rome withdrew"], correct: 2,
      explain: "Chedworth, Woodchester, Bignor and Lullingstone are late, which complicates any simple story of decline." },
  ],
},
);

CHAPTERS.push(
{
  id: "brit-civil", set: "ancient-britain", act: "The Long Decline", title: "A Province Worth Fearing", era: "AD 193 – 212", minutes: 7,
  intro: "Three legions at the end of the world turned out to be a standing temptation, and Rome redesigned the province to make it safe from its own garrison.",
  beats: [
    { title: "Clodius Albinus takes the army to Gaul",
      text: ["When Commodus was murdered at the end of 192 the empire produced five emperors in a year. One of the claimants was **Clodius Albinus**, the governor of Britain, and he mattered for a simple reason: Britain held three legions and a very large auxiliary establishment — something close to a tenth of the entire Roman army, in a province at the far edge of the world with no external enemy worth that many men except the one on the other side of the Wall.",
        "**Septimius Severus** bought him off first, giving him the title of Caesar and the implication of succession while he dealt with rivals in the east. In 196, with the east settled, Albinus was no longer needed. He declared himself Augustus, gathered the British army, and crossed to Gaul.",
        "The two sides met near **Lugdunum** — Lyon — in February 197, in what ancient writers describe as the largest battle ever fought between Roman armies. Albinus lost and died. The soldiers who did not come back were the garrison of Britain, and the north noticed."],
      key: "Albinus, governor of Britain, stripped the province's garrison for a bid for the throne and lost at Lugdunum in 197." },
    { title: "The province gets cut in half",
      text: ["Severus' answer was structural rather than punitive, and it tells you exactly what he thought the problem was. He divided Britain into two provinces — **Britannia Superior**, governed from London with two legions, and **Britannia Inferior**, governed from York with one.",
        "The point was arithmetic. No governor of Britain would ever again have three legions under one command, and therefore no governor of Britain would ever again be able to make a serious attempt on the throne without first persuading a colleague.",
        "It is worth stating plainly what that implies. For the imperial government, the danger presented by Britain was not the Picts. It was the army stationed there to watch the Picts. The frontier required the troops, the troops made the province a launching pad, and Rome spent the next two centuries managing that contradiction — a diagnosis that turns out to be correct, since almost every later crisis in Britain begins with a commander in Britain deciding he should be emperor."],
      key: "Severus split Britain into two provinces so that no governor would command three legions at once." },
    { title: "Everyone becomes Roman",
      text: ["Severus died at **York** in 211 after his failed northern campaign, and his son **Caracalla** issued, in **212**, the most consequential piece of paper in the history of Roman Britain: the **Constitutio Antoniniana**, granting Roman citizenship to almost every free inhabitant of the empire.",
        "Before it, a Briton could become a citizen through military service, magistracy or grant. After it, a farmer in Norfolk who had never seen a Roman official was, in law, as Roman as a senator. The great division of the empire — citizen and provincial — was abolished at a stroke.",
        "The motive was probably not generosity. **Cassius Dio**, writing within a generation, says flatly that it was done to increase revenue, since several taxes fell only on citizens, and that Caracalla simultaneously raised the inheritance tax. Both things can be true: a fiscal measure with a genuinely transformative effect.",
        "Dio also preserves, from the same period, the closest thing to a British woman's recorded speech. During Severus' northern campaign the empress **Julia Domna** is said to have teased the wife of a Caledonian chief named Argentocoxos about the free way British women consorted with men — and to have been told that British women satisfy the demands of nature openly with the best men, whereas Roman women let themselves be debauched in secret by the worst. It is reported by a Roman senator to make a point about Roman morals, which is what happens to every British voice in this Set. It is still, on any reading, a better exchange than the one Rome's version of the conversation would have produced."],
      key: "Caracalla's edict of 212 made almost every free Briton a Roman citizen, probably for tax reasons." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "Why did Severus divide Britain into two provinces?", options: ["To improve tax collection", "So no governor would command three legions and be able to bid for the throne", "To separate Britons from Romans", "To shorten the frontier"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Rome's problem in Britain was its own garrison, not the enemy the garrison was there to watch." },
    { q: "What did the Constitutio Antoniniana of 212 do?", options: ["Ended the province", "Made almost every free inhabitant of the empire a Roman citizen", "Banned druidism", "Divided Britain in two"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Dio says the motive was revenue, since several taxes fell only on citizens." },
  ],
},

{
  id: "brit-breakaway", set: "ancient-britain", act: "The Long Decline", title: "The Empires That Broke Away", era: "AD 260 – 296", minutes: 7,
  intro: "For nearly a quarter of the third century Britain was governed from somewhere other than Rome — and still called itself Roman.",
  beats: [
    { title: "The Gallic Empire",
      text: ["In **260** the emperor Valerian was captured by the Persians, the frontiers gave way in several places at once, and the western provinces stopped waiting for help. An officer on the Rhine named **Postumus** was proclaimed emperor by his troops, and Gaul, Spain and **Britain** went with him.",
        "What followed is often called a rebellion, and it was not quite. Postumus did not march on Rome or claim the whole empire. He ran a fully equipped Roman state in the west — consuls, senate, praetorian guard, coinage in his own name — for the practical reason that the west needed defending and the central government was in no position to do it. Britain was part of that separate Roman empire for fourteen years.",
        "It ended in **274** when Aurelian reabsorbed the west, and it left almost no visible trace in Britain except coins. But it established something: the western provinces had discovered they could function without Rome, and had found the experience survivable."],
      key: "Britain belonged to the breakaway Gallic Empire from 260 to 274, a functioning Roman state run from the west." },
    { title: "Carausius",
      text: ["In **286** a Menapian officer named **Carausius**, commanding the fleet based at Boulogne against Frankish and Saxon raiders in the Channel, was accused of letting the raiders through and confiscating the loot after the fact rather than protecting the coast. Ordered executed, he took the fleet, crossed to Britain, and declared himself emperor.",
        "He held Britain and a strip of northern Gaul for seven years, and he understood publicity better than almost any Roman usurper. His coinage is of unusually high quality at a time when imperial silver was debased, and some issues show him alongside Diocletian and Maximian with the legend **CARAVSIVS ET FRATRES SVI** — Carausius and his brothers — which is a claim to be a legitimate third colleague rather than a rebel.",
        "The remarkable ones carry the letters **RSR** and, on a medallion, **INPCDA**. Those were unexplained for centuries until it was noticed that they expand into a line and a half of **Virgil's** Fourth Eclogue: *Redeunt Saturnia Regna, Iam Nova Progenies Caelo Demittitur Alto* — the reign of Saturn returns, now a new generation is sent down from heaven above. A usurper in Britain was quoting the most famous golden-age prophecy in Latin literature on his money, and expecting somebody to get it.",
        "He was murdered in 293 by his finance minister **Allectus**, who ruled for three more years."],
      key: "Carausius ruled Britain from 286 to 293 with high-quality coinage quoting Virgil's golden-age prophecy." },
    { title: "The restorer of the eternal light",
      text: ["**Constantius Chlorus** recovered Britain in **296** with a two-pronged invasion that got through in fog, missing Allectus' fleet entirely. Allectus was defeated and killed somewhere in the south, and a detachment that had become separated arrived at London in time to catch his Frankish mercenaries sacking it.",
        "The propaganda that followed is preserved on a gold medallion found at **Arras**. It shows Constantius on horseback arriving at a gate, with a kneeling figure labelled **LON** — London — reaching up to him, and the legend **REDDITOR LVCIS AETERNAE**: restorer of the eternal light. A city personified as a suppliant, and the recovery of a province presented as the return of daylight.",
        "The coastal forts built and strengthened through this period — the chain later called the **Saxon Shore**, from Brancaster round to Portchester — are usually explained as defences against seaborne raiding, and that is probably right, though some have argued they are as much about controlling trade and troop movement as repelling attack.",
        "The thing to take from these forty years is what kind of independence it was. Britain was detached from Rome twice and did not once try to stop being Roman. There was no revival of British kingship, no rejection of Latin, no return of the old gods to public life. The men who ran a separate Britain issued Roman coins with Latin legends and quoted Virgil. Whatever the island had been in AD 43, by AD 300 the alternative to being governed from Rome was being governed from London by someone doing an impression of Rome."],
      key: "Constantius recovered Britain in 296; the Arras medallion shows London kneeling to the restorer of the eternal light." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "What did Carausius put on his coinage?", options: ["British tribal symbols", "Lines from Virgil's Fourth Eclogue about a returning golden age", "Christian symbols", "Nothing but his portrait"], correct: 1,
      explain: "RSR and INPCDA expand into the golden-age prophecy, on the money of a usurper at the edge of the world." },
    { q: "What did the breakaway periods show about Britain?", options: ["It wanted independence from Roman culture", "Detached twice, it never stopped being Roman — the alternative was an impression of Rome run from London", "It reverted to tribal kingship", "It allied with the Picts"], correct: 1,
      explain: "No revival of British kingship, no rejection of Latin, no return of the old gods to public life." },
  ],
},
);

CHAPTERS.push(
{
  id: "brit-constantine", set: "ancient-britain", act: "The Long Decline", title: "Constantine, and a Christian Province", era: "AD 306 – 383", minutes: 7,
  intro: "The emperor who legalised Christianity was proclaimed in York, and the richest century of Roman Britain was its last.",
  beats: [
    { title: "Proclaimed at York",
      text: ["**Constantius Chlorus** came back to Britain in 305 to campaign in the north, and died at **York** on 25 July **306**. His son was with him, and the army at York proclaimed **Constantine** emperor on the spot — an irregular succession that took eighteen years and several civil wars to make good.",
        "It is worth stating what that means. The man who would end the persecutions, summon the Council of Nicaea, found Constantinople and set the Roman state on the road to becoming a Christian one began his reign in a legionary fortress in northern England, raised by a provincial garrison. Britain was a backwater in every account written from Rome; it produced the most consequential emperor after Augustus.",
        "The later story that his mother **Helena** was British is a medieval invention — Geoffrey of Monmouth made her a king's daughter from Colchester, which is why Colchester has been claiming her ever since. She came from Asia Minor. Classified **Traditional / Legendary**."],
      key: "Constantine was proclaimed emperor by the garrison at York in 306; the claim that Helena was British is medieval invention." },
    { title: "A Christian province, and a stubbornly pagan one",
      text: ["Britain's Christianity is attested early and thinly. Three British bishops — of **London**, **York** and probably **Lincoln** — attended the Council of **Arles** in 314, with a priest and a deacon, which means an organised provincial church with a diocesan structure existed within a year or two of toleration.",
        "The material evidence is small and remarkable. The **Water Newton** hoard from Cambridgeshire is a set of silver vessels and votive plaques with the Chi-Rho monogram, and is the earliest group of Christian liturgical silver known from anywhere in the Roman empire. At **Lullingstone** in Kent a room in a villa was converted into a house-church with painted figures at prayer. At **Hinton St Mary** in Dorset a mosaic floor carries a beardless male head with the Chi-Rho behind it, widely identified as Christ — which would make it one of the earliest representations anywhere, and raises the awkward question of a Christ laid in a floor to be walked on. The identification is **Probable** rather than certain.",
        "Meanwhile the old gods carried on. The temple to **Nodens** at **Lydney** was built in the 360s, half a century after Constantine, and was a busy healing sanctuary with a guest-house and bath suite. Mithraea, Sulis at Bath and dozens of local deities continued. Fourth-century Britain was not a Christian country with pagan survivals; it was a mixed religious landscape in which Christianity was one option gaining ground unevenly.",
        "And it exported the century's most disruptive theologian. **Pelagius** — a Briton, by the testimony of his enemies — argued in Rome that human beings can choose good without a prior gift of grace, and that if God commands something it must be possible to do it. Augustine spent years demolishing him, the Church condemned him, and the doctrine of original sin took the shape it did partly in reaction. The most influential thinker Roman Britain produced is known almost entirely through the writings of the man who beat him."],
      key: "British bishops attended Arles in 314 and the Water Newton silver is the empire's earliest Christian plate; paganism continued alongside." },
    { title: "The richest century",
      text: ["The fourth century is when Roman Britain looks most prosperous, which is inconvenient for any tidy story of decline. The great villas are late: **Woodchester**, **Chedworth**, **Bignor**, **North Leigh**, with mosaics from workshops good enough that their styles can be traced from site to site. The silver treasures are late: **Mildenhall**, with its great dish of Oceanus and dancing figures; **Hoxne**, buried in the early fifth century with fifteen thousand coins and gold body-chain.",
        "The province was also feeding the empire's armies. When the emperor **Julian** needed grain on the Rhine in the 350s he had it shipped from Britain, building a fleet of hundreds of vessels to do it, which implies a surplus large enough to matter to imperial logistics.",
        "So the province that was about to stop being a province was, in the middle of the fourth century, exporting food, building the largest houses in its history and burying more silver than at any other time. Prosperity and fragility were not alternatives. The wealth was concentrated, dependent on an imperial economy for its markets and its coinage, and defended by an army that could be removed by anyone who wanted it for something else — which is precisely what happened next."],
      key: "The fourth century produced Britain's greatest villas and silver hoards and fed the Rhine armies, shortly before the province ended." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "Where was Constantine proclaimed emperor?", options: ["Rome", "York, by the garrison, on his father's death in 306", "Trier", "Constantinople"], correct: 1,
      explain: "The most consequential emperor after Augustus began his reign in a legionary fortress in northern England." },
    { q: "What does the fourth century tell us about Roman Britain?", options: ["It was already collapsing", "It was at its richest — the great villas, the silver hoards and grain exports are all late", "It had been abandoned by Rome", "It was entirely Christian"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Prosperity and fragility were not alternatives; the wealth depended on an imperial economy and a removable army." },
  ],
},

{
  id: "brit-end", set: "ancient-britain", act: "The Long Decline", title: "The End, Which Was Not an Event", era: "AD 367 – 450", minutes: 8,
  intro: "Roman Britain did not fall in 410. It stopped being paid for, over about thirty years, and nobody wrote down the moment it ended.",
  beats: [
    { title: "367: everything at once",
      text: ["In **367** the frontier failed in a way that had not happened before. **Ammianus Marcellinus** describes Picts, Scotti from Ireland and Attacotti attacking simultaneously while Franks and Saxons raided the Gallic and British coasts, and says the coordination was deliberate. The **Count of the Saxon Shore** was killed and the **dux Britanniarum** cut off; troops deserted; the countryside was plundered for months.",
        "Rome sent **Count Theodosius**, who restored the province over two campaigning seasons, recovered the towns, offered deserters an amnesty and rebuilt the frontier commands. The province was put back together, and it kept working for another generation.",
        "What the episode showed was how thin the margin had become. A frontier that could be undone by a coordinated raid, and restored only by a general and troops sent from outside, is a frontier being held on credit."],
      key: "The coordinated attacks of 367 broke the frontier commands; Count Theodosius restored the province over two seasons." },
    { title: "The army leaves in instalments",
      text: ["Britain's garrison did not withdraw. It was spent, in three or four withdrawals, each by a commander who wanted it for a war somewhere else.",
        "In **383** **Magnus Maximus**, a general in Britain, was proclaimed emperor by his troops and took a large part of the army to Gaul, where he ruled the west for five years before being defeated. Welsh tradition remembered him as **Macsen Wledig** and made him the founder of dynasties, which is the only case in this Set of a Roman being absorbed into British legend rather than the reverse.",
        "Around **402** more troops went to the Rhine and Italy for **Stilicho's** wars. It is at almost exactly this point that new imperial coin stops arriving in Britain in quantity — the great bulk of coins found here end with issues of the 390s — and a province whose economy ran on coin paid to soldiers had lost the mechanism that put money into it.",
        "In **407** a soldier raised in Britain and named, hopefully, **Constantine III** was proclaimed by what remained of the army, crossed to Gaul with it, and never came back. After that there was no field army in Britain, and there was nobody left in Britain who could have summoned one."],
      key: "Magnus Maximus in 383, Stilicho around 402 and Constantine III in 407 each took troops out; none returned." },
    { title: "410, and why it is the wrong date",
      text: ["The date everyone learns comes from a single sentence in the Greek historian **Zosimus**, who says that the emperor **Honorius** wrote to the cities of Britain telling them to look to their own defence.",
        "There are two problems. The first is context: the passage sits in the middle of a discussion of events in Italy, and a respectable body of scholarship holds that the text is corrupt and should read **Bruttium**, in southern Italy, rather than **Britannia**. The second is that even taken at face value it is a reply, not a proclamation — the cities had written asking for help, and were told there was none. That is an administrative shrug, not an act of withdrawal.",
        "The app classifies the Rescript of Honorius as **Contested**, and treats 410 as a convenience. Nothing ended in that year that had not been ending since 402 and would not go on ending until about 430.",
        "What the end actually looked like is visible in the ground rather than the texts. Coin stops arriving and is not replaced by anything. Within a generation the mass-produced pottery industries — which had supplied the whole province with cheap standardised wares for three centuries — simply stop, and people go back to hand-made local pots or to none. Towns thin out, though some are still occupied. Villas are abandoned, and a few have hearths built on their mosaic floors by people using the shell of a house they were not maintaining. The material world of a Roman province unwinds in about two generations, and no source describes it happening."],
      key: "The Rescript of Honorius may not refer to Britain at all; the real end is visible archaeologically as coin and pottery supply stopping." },
    { title: "The island gets its voice back",
      text: ["The people did not go anywhere. Britain in 450 held roughly the same population as Britain in 400, speaking Brittonic and some Latin, farming the same fields. What had gone was the state: no taxes, no army, no coin, no governors, and no way for a town to compel anything from a countryside that no longer had to obey it.",
        "Two voices survive from the wreck, and both are Christian, which is not an accident: the Church was the only institution that came through.",
        "**Gildas**, writing perhaps a century later, produced *On the Ruin of Britain*, and it is not a history. It is a sermon by a furious clergyman arguing that the calamity was God's punishment for the sins of British rulers, whom he names and abuses. He preserves one extraordinary fragment: an appeal to the Roman commander **Aetius**, which he calls the groans of the Britons, saying the barbarians drive us to the sea and the sea drives us back to the barbarians, and between the two we are either killed or drowned. No help came.",
        "The other is **Patrick**. A Briton from a Romanised, Christian, decurion family, kidnapped by Irish raiders at sixteen, enslaved for six years, escaped, and went back to Ireland as a missionary. His *Confessio* is a defence of his ministry written to British churchmen who were attacking him, in awkward Latin he apologises for, and it is the first autobiography written in these islands — a real person explaining himself, in his own words, with no Roman intermediary at all.",
        "That is where this Set ends, and it is a strange symmetry. Britain enters the written record as a rumour of tin islands that Herodotus admits he cannot verify, and spends four thousand years being described by visitors, traders, generals and their sons-in-law. The first inhabitant of the island to speak to us directly and at length is a runaway slave apologising for his grammar."],
      key: "The state ended, not the population; Gildas' sermon and Patrick's Confessio are the first British voices to survive." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "Why is 410 a poor date for the end of Roman Britain?", options: ["The Romans left in 350", "The Rescript of Honorius may refer to Bruttium, and it was a refusal of help rather than a withdrawal", "Britain was never Roman", "The date comes from Gildas"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Nothing ended that year that had not been ending since 402 and would not continue until about 430." },
    { q: "What is Patrick's Confessio?", options: ["A history of Roman Britain", "A defence of his ministry, and the first autobiography written in these islands", "A legal document", "A saint's life written later"], correct: 1,
      explain: "The first inhabitant of Britain to speak to us directly and at length is a former slave apologising for his Latin." },
  ],
},
);
